  "text": "הבנה:\n1. To find the point (x, y) inside the parallelogram ABCD such that the four triangles formed with vertices at this point and two adjacent vertices of the parallelogram have equal perimeters, you need to use the properties of similar triangles formed by the diagonals of the parallelogram which intersect at that point.\n\n2. In a parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other, meaning they cross at their midpoints. This means that if we draw the diagonals, they will intersect at a central point where the distances from this point to each side are equal.\n\n3. This central point is often referred to as the bicentric center of the parallelogram, which implies that the point which divides the parallelogram into four triangles of equal area and perimeter (or half-angle) is the same point.\n\n4. In a parallelogram with vertices (2,3), (6,5), (8,9), and (4,7), we can see that the central point of the parallelogram has coordinates ((2+8)/2, (3+9)/2) = (5,6).\n\n5. This central point also divides the parallelogram into four triangles with equal perimeters, as it is the point where the diagonals intersect.\n\n6. We need to find the value of 5x + 2y for the point (x, y), which we have determined to be (5,6) from the properties of the parallelogram.\n\n∴ 5x + 2y = 5(5) + 2(6) = 25 + 12 = 37",
